A small proportion of rain evaporates directly from a plant’s surface, even during rainfall;   
In many trees, water is collected in the upper canopies and flows down eventually into the soil;   
During rainfall, water is stored on the surface of leaves and stems, gradually making its way into the soil;   
During the growth of vegetation, water is removed from the soil by   
transpiration, where the water is then evaporated from the plants into the atmosphere, replenishing the original source of rainfall;   
Trees with large leaf areas and deep roots have higher rates of   
transpiration; and   
Transpiration helps to dry out soils; during rain, water will infiltrate more readily into dry soil.

